import { bankUnit, type Q } from "../own";

// Grade 7 science and social studies, Prince Edward Island: Earth’s Crust (rocks and minerals, weathering and erosion)
// and the five contexts of empowerment and disempowerment in Canada, 1830 to 1920. The PEI Grade 7 social studies guide
// was not available, so the history here is well-documented national history only, matched to each context.
// Indigenous content is light, in the present tense for living communities, and needs review by Mi’kmaq partners (for
// example the Mi’kmaq Confederacy of PEI) and the PEI Department of Education before launch. The residential school
// system is stated briefly, without graphic detail, and needs the same review.
//
// Sources checked:
// The Canadian Encyclopedia, "Chinese Head Tax" and "Anti-Chinese Immigration Policies in Canada" (thecanadianencyclopedia.ca)
// The Canadian Encyclopedia, "Indian Act", "Gradual Civilization Act" and "Gradual Enfranchisement Act" (thecanadianencyclopedia.ca)
// The Canadian Encyclopedia, "women’s suffrage in Canada" (thecanadianencyclopedia.ca)
// Elections Canada, "Background information: Women and the Vote" (electionsanddemocracy.ca)
// Standard textbook facts for rocks, minerals, the rock cycle, the Mohs scale, weathering, erosion and deposition
// Standard Canadian history references for responsible government, Confederation, the Rebellions of 1837-38, the Charlottetown
//   Conference (1864), PEI’s entry in 1873, the Trade Unions Act (1872), the secret ballot (1874) and the National Policy (1879)
// Left out because unverified: exact tonnage or counts for PEI shipbuilding, numbers of Chinese railway workers, the dates of
//   PEI-specific school or labour laws, Mi’kmaq community histories on Epekwitk, the number of children in residential
//   schools, and the PEI date for women holding office.

